  "spans": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "nodeId": "n2",
      "text": "DOES THE LIVESTOCK SECTOR CONSTITUTE 51% OF WORLDWIDE GREENHOUSE GAS EMISSIONS?",
      "contextBefore": "y of Environment [8] were overestimated by more than 4 times compared to the RDA data (The Ministry of Environment estimated 2.99 tons GHG/head/yr, while the RDA estimated around 600 kg GHG/head/yr). ",
      "contextAfter": " In 2009, the Worldwatch Institute [17], a private environmental research institute, released a report claiming that the livestock sector was responsible for 51% of total GHG emissions. This assertion",
      "start": 10897,
      "end": 10976,
      "extractSha256": "a44dade593407dd87dae1901a50fccaf8c1bb8e2498f49860d6c8e7a6a47510e",
      "windowSha256": "6c59fb9430279531c6fd15f2441b88363ae44553f118a2e39fb7804ba9db5077",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-17T06:23:39.880Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20250117023656/https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10915186",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "nodeId": "n3",
      "text": "Of marketed livestock production by value, about 51% consists of stock for slaughter and 35% of dairy products.",
      "contextBefore": "keting. Although they account for less than 10% of export earnings, livestock products are important domestically, contributing 23% of the gross value of marketed agricultural production (Table 9.4). ",
      "contextAfter": " The remainder is divided among hides, skins, wool and eggs. However, most of the livestock industry is outside the money economy since 75% of milk production, 80% of beef and over 90% of goat and she",
      "start": 1959,
      "end": 2070,
      "extractSha256": "9538771f4b9bc6c1a49a9988275ed160f849fab5c3376efd6f6dc020cc80022a",
      "windowSha256": "058c59c3a035237898874653f0f5e1dccc6a59f3b60fa2906b0d52a0efb59a31",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-17T06:23:40.841Z",
      "waybackUrl": null,
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "nodeId": "n4",
      "text": "In the November/December 2009 issue of the *World Watch* journal, Goodland and co-author Jeff Anhang published the shocking estimate of \"at least 51%\" of global greenhouse gas emissions attributable to the lifecycle and supply chain of livestock products (meaning all meats, dairy, and by-products).",
      "contextBefore": "ronmental conservation. He is the co-author of *Amazon Jungle-Green Hell to Red Desert?: An Ecological Discussion of the Environmental Impact of the Highway Construction Program in the Amazon Basin*. ",
      "contextAfter": " PROGRAM TRANSCRIPTION CH: This is Robert Goodland. He is retired as the lead Environmental Advisor at the World Bank group and he served there for 23 years. In 2008 he was awarded the IUCN's first Co",
      "start": 604,
      "end": 903,
      "extractSha256": "adfbf5a2bad84d6af25b5a33fad6cc1340c7f33eb68b6223cc227a5865b56960",
      "windowSha256": "a26152c15c358b559e6f82372c2dd06afb7e95a6279fdc311957feaf60857c94",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-17T06:23:53.970Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20150829005126/https://responsibleeatingandliving.com/favorites/robert-goodland-interview",
      "tier": "exact"
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "nodeId": "n5",
      "text": "A more recent and comprehensive study published by Worldwatch Institute, however, finds that livestock and their byproducts actually account for 51% of worldwide human-related greenhouse gas emissions annually.",
      "contextBefore": "ilable for grazing. [[2]](#_ftnref2) Henning Steinfeld et al., FAO, Livestock's Long Shadow: Environmental Issues and Options (2006), *available at* http://www.fao.org/docrep/010/a0701e/a0701e00.htm. ",
      "contextAfter": " Robert Goodland & Jeff Anhang, *Livestock and Climate Change*, World Watch Magazine, November/December 2009, at 11. [[3]](#_ftnref3) David Tilman & Michael Clark, *Global Diets Link Environmental Sus",
      "start": 12307,
      "end": 12517,
      "extractSha256": "882cfd8a1344518b8417e4381b1ed0c833a9b5631ca53174be6f05ed1d4ebe4f",
      "windowSha256": "8bda944ade6f76dfcdf8c8a1a3fc427144777e9b020f7e3b8444d6b3a053acbc",
      "fetchedAt": "2026-11-17T06:24:02.262Z",
      "waybackUrl": "https://web.archive.org/web/20251103054331/https://journals.law.harvard.edu/elr/2015/10/26/elrs-a-leading-cause-of-everything-one-industry-that-is-destroying-our-planet-and-our-ability-to-thrive-on-it",
      "tier": "exact"
    }
